+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole
+number of seconds, like `120`, or one or more groups of an integer followed by a unit: `d` for days, `h` for hours, `m`
+for minutes, and `s` for seconds. Each unit can be given at most once, units must be in that order, and spaces are not
+allowed. Units are case-insensitive, so `1h30m` and `1H30M` are the same. Anything else, such as negative or decimal
+numbers, fails with an error.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="5m", show_default=True)
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Timeout: {timeout}")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+```
+
